    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d504">

                <pb facs="0762" n="748" sameAs="frus:frus1946v06/pg748"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20200(D)/4–2446: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Smith</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-04-24T11:00:00+03:00">April 24, 1946—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 24—8:39 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1312. For Benton from Smith. Lozovski informed me by letter dated April 23 that
                    Central Organization for Delivery of Newspapers and Magazines throughout USSR
                        (Soyuzpechat) will undertake
                    distribution of 50,000 copies of illustrated magazine <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >America</hi> beginning June 1 this year. Hope this news, which means <hi
                        rendition="#italic">America</hi> can operate without loss, will persuade
                    Congress to permit continuance of magazine.<anchor n="88" xml:id="d504-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Smith</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d504-f1" n="88" xml:id="d504-f1-content">
                <p>A Department of State press release of August 30, 1946, announced the permission
                    for this increase in circulation at 10 rubles a copy; Department of State <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, September 15, 1946, pp. 513–514. The
                    Committee on Appropriations of the House of Representatives had temporarily
                    favored discontinuance of the magazine because of its limited distribution and
                    cost of publication.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
